.TH dc "" "Command"
.SH NAME
dc \- desk calculator
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBdc [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
dc -- desk calculator
Usage:	dc [ file ]
Arbitrary precision desk calculator with registers,
using reverse Polish notation.
Reads input from 'file' if given, then from standard input.
.HE
.B dc
is an arbitrary-precision reverse-Polish desk calculator with 256
registers, each of which is itself a small stack.
If
.I file
is given,
.B dc
reads and executes it first; it then reads and executes the standard
input.
An arbitrary number of commands may appear per line, and commands
need not be separated by spaces.
.PP
Numbers are pushed onto a value stack; operators pop their operands
from that stack and push the result.
A number is a string of the digits
.B 0
through
.B 9
and the letters
.B A
through
.BR F ,
optionally containing one decimal point; the letters stand for the
values ten through fifteen regardless of the input base currently in
effect.
An underscore (\fB_\fR) immediately before a number makes it
negative.
Every value carries its own
.IR scale " (" "number of digits after the decimal point" );
.B dc
keeps the natural scale through addition, subtraction, and
multiplication, but division and exponentiation follow the rules
given under
.B k
below.
.PP
.B dc
recognizes the following commands:
.IP "\fB+ \-  * / % ^\fR" 0.9i
Pop the top two values, apply addition, subtraction, multiplication,
division, remainder, or exponentiation (in that order), and push the
result.
For
.BR ^ ,
the exponent's fractional part, if any, is ignored.
.IP \fBc\fR
Clear (discard) the entire stack.
.IP \fBd\fR
Duplicate the top of the stack.
.IP \fBf\fR
Print every register that has a value, followed by the entire stack.
.IP \fBi\fR
Pop the top of the stack and use its integer part as the input base
(digits are taken from
.BR 0 - F ,
so the input base is normally between 2 and 16).
.IP \fBI\fR
Push the current input base.
.IP \fBk\fR
Pop the top of the stack and use its integer part as the scale factor
used by division; a negative value is rejected and the scale is left
at zero.
.IP \fBK\fR
Push the current scale factor.
.IP "\fBl\fIx\fR"
Push a copy of the top of register
.IR x ;
the register is left unchanged.
.IP "\fBL\fIx\fR"
Pop the top of register
.I x
and push it (removing it from the register).
.IP \fBo\fR
Pop the top of the stack and use its integer part as the output base.
.IP \fBO\fR
Push the current output base.
.IP \fBp\fR
Print the top of the stack; the value is left on the stack.
.IP \fBP\fR
Pop the top of the stack and print it.
.IP \fBq\fR
Quit
.BR dc .
.IP "\fBQ\fIn\fR"
Pop the top of the stack and use its integer part as
.IR n ;
exit
.I n
levels of string execution begun with
.BR x .
.IP "\fBs\fIx\fR"
Pop the top of the stack and store it in register
.I x
(any previous top-of-register value in
.I x
is discarded).
.IP "\fBS\fIx\fR"
Pop the top of the stack and push it onto register
.IR x ,
preserving whatever
.I x
already held beneath it.
.IP \fBv\fR
Replace the top of the stack with its square root.
.IP \fBx\fR
Pop the top of the stack; if it is a bracketed string (see
.B [ ]
below), execute it as a sequence of
.B dc
commands.
Popping a plain number has no further effect.
.IP \fBX\fR
Replace the top of the stack with its scale (the number of digits
after its decimal point).
.IP \fBz\fR
Push the current number of values on the stack.
.IP \fBZ\fR
Replace the top of the stack with the number of digits in its
printed representation.
.IP \fB[...]\fR
Push the enclosed character string, which may later be run with
.BR x ,
with \fBl\fR/\fBL\fR and a register, or as the action of a comparison
(below).
Brackets may be nested.
.IP "\fB<\fIx\fB >\fIx\fB =\fIx\fB !<\fIx\fB !>\fIx\fB !=\fIx\fR"
Pop the top two values and compare them.
If the (optionally negated) relation holds, execute the top of
register
.I x
as with
.BR x .
.IP \fB?\fR
Read a line from the standard input (regardless of where
.B dc
is currently reading its program from) and execute it as a string of
.B dc
commands.
.IP \fB!\fR
Read the rest of the input line and pass it to the shell; when the
shell command finishes,
.B dc
prints
.B !
and resumes reading commands.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On a stack-empty, stack-full, out-of-memory, missing-register-name,
negative-scale, or unrecognized-command error,
.B dc
prints a one-line message to the standard error and abandons the
command it was in the middle of, but continues reading input
immediately after the offending character.
.SH NOTES
For most purposes the infix notation of
.B bc
is more convenient than the reverse-Polish notation of
.BR dc .
